1	# Roadmap
2	
3	Roadmap order is risk-driven. Stable-release dates remain intentionally unset until the beta is
4	exercised on external repositories.
5	
6	## 0.1 beta — deterministic project memory and handoff
7	
8	Implemented in the `0.1.0` beta line:
9	
10	- versioned canonical configuration with a public v1 JSON Schema and compatibility contract;
11	- progressive always/on-demand context catalog with explicit budgets;
12	- registry-backed Codex and Claude adapters with golden fixtures and non-destructive adoption;
13	- normalized ownership, traversal, symlink, bounded-read, marker, and drift validation;
14	- staged atomic file replacement with stale-plan detection and temporary cleanup;
15	- deterministic Git handoff evidence: branch/HEAD, divergence, status, numstat, paths, commits;
16	- hostile Git environment, process deadline/output, invalid filename, and fsmonitor controls;
17	- human CLI contracts for every command and JSON contracts for `validate` and `handoff`;
18	- deterministic fuzz/property, performance, adoption, package, and three-platform CI design;
19	- exact clean-build npm artifact and local/ephemeral/global/ESM/TypeScript smoke paths;
20	- the historical `@jaemani/agent-context-kit@0.1.0-beta.3` public beta with registry digests,
21	  provenance, one-off execution, global installation, initialization, and validation evidence;
22	- Carrylog product/package/CLI identity with a tested beta.3 repository compatibility path that
23	  preserves configuration v1 wire identifiers.
24	
25	Completed publication and channel-hardening operations:
26	
27	- published and verified `carrylog@0.1.0-beta.4` from the renamed repository;
28	- configured protected trusted publishing and retired the short-lived bootstrap path;
29	- keep the registry-required first-publication `latest` fixed at beta.4 until stable while later
30	  prereleases advance only through explicit `@beta` opt-in;
31	- deprecated the old scoped beta with an exact Carrylog migration message;
32	- published and verified beta.5 through protected OIDC with no registry token.
33	
34	Delivery and outcome work now takes priority over additional defensive breadth:
35	
36	- exercise the published five-minute registry path on external repositories;
37	- run the committed Tier 1 checkpoint-continuity pilot before building session journaling;
38	- dogfood on two additional materially different repositories and publish sanitized adoption notes;
39	- measure whether users reduce project re-explanation and repeated work;
40	- revisit positioning and adapter assumptions from evidence rather than registry or standards trends
41	  alone.
42	
43	Implemented in the `0.1.0-beta.5` prerelease line:
44	
45	- explicit configuration v2 migration without changing frozen v1 semantics;
46	- shared Codex/Cursor `agents` surface plus Claude Code and Gemini CLI root routers;
47	- repository continuity Skills with fail-closed ownership;
48	- deterministic checkpoint/resume envelope with guarded consistent reads and no transcript parsing.
49	- token-free protected OIDC publication with complete prerelease dist-tag and public-artifact
50	  verification.
51	
52	## 0.2 beta — measured adoption and selective adapter breadth
53	
54	- authenticated Cursor fresh-session conformance and GitHub Copilot research only where adoption
55	  demand justifies another surface;
56	- nested instruction outputs only where official precedence behavior and a user job are documented;
57	- CI policy mode and a compact, deterministic project-card export for session-journal consumers;
58	- measure the implemented tool-neutral checkpoint/resume contract without claiming transcript or
59	  hidden-state reproduction;
60	- promote the Tier 1 protocol to independent Tier 2 only if the pilot shows useful signal;
61	- opt-in measurement of reconstruction time, repeated work, handoff usefulness, and user perception;
62	- external adoption reports from at least three materially different repositories, beginning before
63	  0.2 feature expansion;
64	- additional Windows hard-link and junction/reparse-point policy and tests.
65	
66	## 0.3 beta — conditional freshness and reversible compaction research
67	
68	- evidence-based document freshness metadata rather than wall-clock claims;
69	- deterministic size reporting by load tier;
70	- archive workflow with reversible moves and link validation;
71	- optional tokenizer plugins without a core model dependency, only if measurement requires them;
72	- no destructive one-shot summarization;
73	- no claim to control or reproduce each agent's internal compaction; reversible external project
74	  memory remains the continuity boundary.
75	
76	Session journaling, semantic compaction, or compact superiority claims do not enter the supported
77	runtime until ADR-0012's staged evidence and privacy gates pass. Native resume and native compaction
78	remain comparison baselines.
79	
80	## Stable gate
81	
82	- beta feedback shows reduced context reconstruction without instruction bloat;
83	- configuration migration implementation exists before a v2 schema is introduced;
84	- supported-platform package and registry paths remain green over multiple releases;
85	- security review has no unresolved high-severity path, Git, package, or plugin findings;
86	- release provenance and rollback/deprecation procedures have been exercised;
87	- public API and CLI compatibility commitments are explicit.
88	
89	## Post-beta exploration
90	
91	- read-first MCP server for context queries and decision search;
92	- optional issue-tracker connectors with explicit provenance;
93	- team policies and dashboards that do not require uploading source context;
94	- extensible adapter/plugin SDK after a safe trust model exists.
